### What is Exchanger?

You create one with `Exchanger<T> ex = new Exchanger<>()`. The only essential method is `exchange(V x)`, which blocks until the other thread also calls exchange, then each thread receives the other's object. The swap is atomic and symmetric.

### How does double buffering work?

Double buffering keeps two buffers alive. The filler fills one while the drainer drains the other. When both are done, they swap buffers and repeat. This keeps both threads busy and avoids contention on a shared buffer.

```java
package com.bank.buffer;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.Exchanger;

public class DoubleBufferDemo {

    private static final int CAPACITY = 100;
    private static final Exchanger<List<String>> exchanger = new Exchanger<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Thread(() -> filler(), "filler").start();
        new Thread(() -> drainer(), "drainer").start();
    }

    static void filler() {
        List<String> buffer = new ArrayList<>(CAPACITY);
        try {
            for (int i = 0; i < 500; i++) {
                buffer.add("Trade-" + i);
                if (buffer.size() == CAPACITY) {
                    buffer = exchanger.exchange(buffer);  // hand full, get empty
                    buffer.clear();
                }
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }

    static void drainer() {
        List<String> buffer = new ArrayList<>(CAPACITY);
        try {
            for (int round = 0; round < 5; round++) {
                buffer = exchanger.exchange(buffer);  // hand empty, get full
                buffer.forEach(trade -> System.out.println("Posted: " + trade));
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }
}
```

### How does the timed variant protect against stalls?

If one thread crashes or stalls, the other blocks forever in exchange(). The timed variant prevents this by throwing TimeoutException after the specified duration.

```java
try {
    buffer = exchanger.exchange(buffer, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
} catch (TimeoutException e) {
    System.err.println("Partner stalled. Raising alert for operations.");
    alertOps("Exchanger partner not responding");
}
```

### How does Exchanger compare to BlockingQueue?

| Feature | Exchanger | BlockingQueue |
|---------|-----------|---------------|
| Threads | Exactly two | Many producers and consumers |
| Direction | Bidirectional swap | One-directional flow |
| Internal storage | None (rendezvous) | Bounded or unbounded buffer |
| Coupling | Tight (both must meet) | Loose (decoupled) |
| Buffer reuse | Yes (two objects bounce) | No (elements flow through) |
| GC pressure | Low (reuse same buffers) | Higher (elements created and discarded) |

### What about memory visibility?

Exchange establishes a happens-before relationship as defined by the Java Memory Model. Everything written to the buffer before calling exchange is visible to the partner after exchange returns. No additional synchronization is needed on the buffer contents.

### How is Exchanger used in a banking trade ingest pipeline?

At National Bank of India, the trade ingest path uses an Exchanger to swap trade lists between a reader thread (reads trades from a network socket) and a writer thread (batches inserts to the database). Two lists are always alive. The reader fills one while the writer drains the other. After swap, both continue with fresh buffers. No garbage churn, no shared-buffer contention.

```java
package com.bank.ingest;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.Exchanger;

public class TradeIngestPipeline {

    private static final int BATCH_SIZE = 200;
    private final Exchanger<List<Trade>> exchanger = new Exchanger<>();

    public void startReader() {
        List<Trade> buffer = new ArrayList<>(BATCH_SIZE);
        while (running) {
            Trade trade = readFromSocket();
            buffer.add(trade);
            if (buffer.size() >= BATCH_SIZE) {
                try {
                    buffer = exchanger.exchange(buffer);
                    buffer.clear();
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    return;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public void startWriter() {
        List<Trade> buffer = new ArrayList<>(BATCH_SIZE);
        while (running) {
            try {
                buffer = exchanger.exchange(buffer);
                batchInsert(buffer);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                return;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using Exchanger with more than two threads leads to unpredictable pairing. Thread A might exchange with Thread B or Thread C, depending on timing. For multi-party exchange, use a different mechanism.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can you use Exchanger for more than two threads? Technically yes, but pairing is non-deterministic and usually incorrect.
- How does double buffering reduce GC pressure? Only two buffer objects are ever created and reused. No intermediate objects are garbage collected.
- When is BlockingQueue a better choice? When you have multiple producers or consumers, or when you want decoupling and buffering between unequally-paced threads.
